 three leptons (referred to as 3 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons), or exactly one lepton (referred to as 1 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons).\"}\n```"}, {"paper_id": "2006.05880", "qid": "gf-06", "answer": true, "quote": "The events are classified into two exclusive categories: at least three leptons (referred to as 3 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons), or exactly one lepton (referred to as 1 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons).", "quote_verified": true, "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The 3 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons.", "section": "Event selection", "window": 2, "escalated": false, "raw": "```json\n{\"found\": true, \"answer\": \"The 3 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons.\", \"quote\": \"The events are classified into two exclusive categories: at least three leptons (referred to as 3 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons), or exactly one lepton (referred to as 1 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons).\"}\n```"}]}
{"qid": "gf-11", "paper_id": "2001.06899", "answer": true, "unanimous": true, "quote": "The algorithm used in the analysis, the combined secondary vertex (Version 2), is described in Ref. [ 42 ] .", "answers": ["The combined secondary vertex (Version 2)", "combined secondary vertex (Version 2)"], "votes": {"False": 4, "True": 2}, "escalated": false, "sections_read": [" + Abstract + Introduction + The CMS detector + Data and simulated samples", "Object reconstruction and event selection"], "verdicts": [{"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-11", "answer": false, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "", "section": " + Abstract + Introduction + The CMS detector + Data and simulated samples", "window": 0, "escalated": false, "raw": "```json\n{\"found\": false, \"answer\": \"\", \"quote\": \"\"}\n```"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-11", "answer": false, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "", "section": " + Abstract + Introduction + The CMS detector + Data and simulated samples", "window": 0, "escalated": false, "raw": "```json\n{\"found\": false, \"answer\": \"\", \"quote\": \"\"}\n```"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-11", "answer": false, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "", "section": " + Abstract + Introduction + The CMS detector + Data and simulated samples", "window": 0, "escalated": false, "raw": "```json\n{\"found\": false, \"answer\": \"\", \"quote\": \"\"}\n```"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-11", "answer": true, "quote": "The algorithm used in the analysis, the combined secondary vertex (Version 2), is described in Ref. [ 42 ] .", "quote_verified": true, "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "combined secondary vertex (Version 2)", "section": "Object reconstruction and event selection", "window": 1, "escalated": false, "raw": "```json\n{\"found\": true, \"answer\": \"combined secondary vertex (Version 2)\", \"quote\": \"The algorithm used in the analysis, the combined secondary vertex (Version 2), is described in Ref. [ 42 ] .\"}\n```"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-11", "answer": true, "quote": "The algorithm used in the analysis, the combined secondary vertex (Version 2), is described in Ref. [ 42 ] .", "quote_verified": true, "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The combined secondary vertex (Version 2)", "section": "Object reconstruction and event selection", "window": 1, "escalated": false, "raw": "```json\n{\"found\": true, \"answer\": \"The combined secondary vertex (Version 2)\", \"quote\": \"The algorithm used in the analysis, the combined secondary vertex (Version 2), is described in Ref. [ 42 ] .\"}\n```"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-11", "answer": null, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "", "section": "Object reconstruction and event selection", "window": 1, "escalated": false, "raw": "```json\n{\"found\": true, \"answer\": \"The combined secondary vertex (Version 2) algorithm is used. The analysis uses a “medium” operating point, which corresponds to approximately 10 (60)% tagging efficiencies for $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ ( $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ ) quark jets and a misidentification probability of 1% for a light jet.\", \"quote\": \"The algorithm used in the analysis, the combined secondary vertex (Version 2), is described in Ref. [ 42 ] . Some of the important input variables are the number of secondary vertices and the number of tracks associated with each of them, the mass and 2D decay distance significance of the SV with the smallest decay distance uncertainty, and the signed 3D impact parameter significance of the tracks. Here the significance is defined as the ratio between a measured quantity and its uncertainty. Although the combined secondary vertex (Version 2) is trained to distinguish $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets, it does occasionally tag a $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jet. Therefore, at a proper operating point, the algorithm can retain a sufficient amount of $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jets while heavily suppressing light jets. The analysis uses a “medium” operating point, which corresponds to approximately 10 (60)% tagging efficiencies for $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ ( $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ ) quark jets and a misidentification probability of 1% for a light jet.\"}\n```"}]}
{"qid": "gf-10", "paper_id": "2001.06899", "answer": false, "unanimous": true, "quote": "", "answers": [], "votes": {"False": 30}, "escalated": false, "sections_read": [" + Abstract + Introduction + The CMS detector + Data and simulated samples", "Appendix B Measured differential cross section ratios", "Cross section ratio measurements + Analysis strategy + $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ +HF jets event tagging efficiency", "Estimation of the event yields", "Object reconstruction and event selection", "Results + Summary + Appendix A Post-fit $M_{\\text{SV}}$ distributions in the exclusive jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins", "Systematic uncertainties", "Unfolding"], "verdicts": [{"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-10", "answer": false, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "", "section": " + Abstract + Introduction + The CMS detector + Data and simulated samples", "window": 0, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"found\": false}"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-10", "answer": false, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "", "section": " + Abstract + Introduction + The CMS detector + Data and simulated samples", "window": 0, "escalated": false, "raw": "```json\n{\"found\": false, \"answer\": \"\", \"quote\": \"\"}\n```"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-10", "answer": false, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "", "section": " + Abstract + Introduction + The CMS detector + Data and simulated samples", "window": 0, "escalated": false, "raw": "```json\n{\"found\": false, \"answer\": \"\", \"quote\": \"\"}\n```"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-10", "answer": null, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "", "section": "Object reconstruction and event selection", "window": 1, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"found\": true, \"answer\": \"80%\", \"quote\": \"Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .\"}"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-10", "answer": null, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "", "section": "Object reconstruction and event selection", "window": 1, "escalated": false, "raw": "```json\n{\"found\": true, \"answer\": \"80%\", \"quote\": \"The missing transverse momentum vector, ${\\vec{p}}_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\kern 1.0pt\\text{miss}}$ , is computed as the negative vector sum of the transverse momenta of all PF candidates in an event [ 41 ] and is further modified to account for corrections to the energy scale of the reconstructed jets. Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .\"}\n```"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-10", "answer": null, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "", "section": "Object reconstruction and event selection", "window": 1, "escalated": false, "raw": "```json\n{\"found\": true, \"answer\": \"80%\", \"quote\": \"which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$.\"}\n```"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-10", "answer": false, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "", "section": "Object reconstruction and event selection", "window": 2, "escalated": false, "raw": "```json\n{\"found\": false, \"answer\": \"\", \"quote\": \"\"}\n```"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-10", "answer": false, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "", "section": "Object reconstruction and event selection", "window": 2, 